# On the Use and Abuse of Logic
## Why arguments for and against materialism actually never prove that it's true or it's false

There are plenty of valid logical arguments that claim to prove or disprove materialism. The reasoning in all of them is sound, yet they produce opposite conclusions. How can this be? A short primer on how inferential logic actually works.

Think about arithmetic. There's only one right way to add numbers, but different numbers give different answers: Start with 2 and 6, and you get 8. Start with -2 and -6, you get -8. So too with logic&mdash;but here the starting point is one or more foundational assumptions: statements taken to be true without proof. Just as you can't add without choosing some numbers first, you can't reason without initial assumptions. Which assumptions you choose are up to you. The only requirement is that you phrase them as a true-or-false statement. *My dog is dead* is a valid assumption for the purposes of logic, whether or not it's true; *my dog* is not, because it can't be either true or false.

Assumptions can be as wild as you like, as long as they fit this true-or-false format: *cats are solar flares emitted from Betelgeuse that solidify as they travel through space*, for example. Starting there, where can you get with logic? Fiction writers do this all the time. If you want answers about actual reality, however, your starting-point assumptions shouldn't contradict established fact. For example, *carbon is always deadly to life* is provably false. The best reasoning in the world won't give you facts about life on earth if you start with that assumption. (Great fiction prompt, though.)

The heated debate over logical worldview proofs is based on two confusions. First, often when people accuse each other of faulty logic, they actually just disagree on foundational assumptions. A more accurate response would be *I see how you reached that conclusion, I just don't agree that premise X is likely to be true*.

Second, people generally confuse their own foundational assumptions for truths. But by definition one's initial assumptions could be true or untrue; they're just a place to start reasoning from. Regardless of how passionately one feels they are true, their truth value is not relevant to the logical argument. People are fuzzy on this. They think they're saying *X is true, and Y logically follows from X, so Y is true. QED!* But that's not how it works. What they're actually saying is *if X is true (and I personally believe it is, based on the world I see around me), then Y is also true, because Y logically follows from X*. 

But, you might say, surely sometimes X *has* been proven true. Let's use a concrete example here, to avoid drowning in the abstract. In worldview arguments, X is usually a scientific result (such as the age of hominid fossil remains based on carbon dating). This X is indeed true under certain assumptions: the half-life of carbon-14 has not changed over time; no process other than radioactive decay removed carbon-14 from the fossil; the amount of carbon-14 originally in this fossil was not drastically different from the expected amount; and so on. Now, I agree that these assumptions are highly plausible&mdash;but what they are not, in logical terms, is *proven*. And they cannot be proven; we don't have and cannot get any data on the original fossil or anything that happened to it before we found it. And so the truth of X is, again, tied to unproven initial assumptions.  

Scientists understand this; it's why they give us Theories and not Facts. No assumption that relies on incomplete data is ever proven true. New data might always come in later that prove the assumption false. (Simple if contrived example: start at the number 1 and count up, testing each one against the theory *all numbers are less than or equal to 13,456,987,231,433,569*. The theory just looks more and more likely... until you hit 13,456,987,231,433,570. Less contrived example: *Newtonian physics perfectly describes the universe* under more precise empirical data.) A theory that doesn't match all-the-data-so-far can't be right, but one that does only ever *might* be. 

This means that while contradicting theories cannot both be true, they might both match all the data we currently have. In that case, we can't say which one (if either) actually represents reality. Either theory could serve as a foundational assumption for a logical argument about the nature of reality, and the most any of us can say is, again, *I see how you arrived at that conclusion, I just don't agree that premise X is likely to be true*.

On to one final point. Materialism is the assumption that the physical universe is all there is. Materialists then tell us that science, the forming of logical conclusions based on empirical data, is the only valid source of knowledge. This is sometimes used to argue against materialism: *that claim itself is not a form of science*. This error has two parts: first, it overlooks the fact that science is defined by the use of reason, not just the empirical data. Second, it reverses the order of the argument, so that one is thought to claim 'only science is valid' in order to prove 'only the physical universe exists'. *What a circular argument*, we complain! *Science is limited to the physical by design*. But the implication works the other way round. If materialism is true, then, as its proponents correctly deduce, science is our only possible source of knowledge. The proper response from a non-materialist is thus *I see how you arrived at that conclusion, I just don't agree that the physical universe is all there is*.
